/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"production-level-2","number":2,"title":"Module 2 — Warp, Chop, and MIDI Law","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"Ableton: “The process of adjusting the timing and rhythm of audio clips is referred to as warping. Warping lets you treat audio as though it were elastic.” You can change timing without pitch, or (in Re-Pitch) change both like tape / a turn","modules":[{"id":"production-m-2-1","code":"2","title":"Part A: Tempo, warp modes, when not to warp","blurb":"Ableton: “The process of adjusting the timing and rhythm of audio clips is referred to as warping. Warping lets you treat audio as though it were elastic.” You can change timing without pitch, or (in Re-Pitch) change both like tape / a turn","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"production-l-o-4","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"overview","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module 2 — Part A: Tempo, warp modes, when not to warp","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Intent:</strong> Make audio <strong>sit in a song</strong>. Ableton still owns every algorithm footnote. This course owns the producer’s decision.   <strong>Required.</strong> Gate A is at the end of <code>Module-01-Chop-MIDI-Law.md</code> (sections 1.4–1.6).   <strong>Do not:</strong> Suite Stem Separation. Do not invent BPMs.   <strong>Primary sources:</strong> Live 12 Standard Manual ch. 9 (<em>Audio Clips, Tempo, and Warping</em>); EDMProd remix tip #10 (Complex / Complex Pro on vocals/stems)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton: “The process of adjusting the timing and rhythm of audio clips is referred to as <strong>warping</strong>. Warping lets you treat audio as though it were elastic.” You can change timing without pitch, or (in Re-Pitch) change both like tape / a turntable."},{"type":"heading","text":"Section 2.1 — Tempo, grid, first downbeat","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Import audio. Set or tap tempo. Put beat 1 on 1.1.1. Treat Auto-Warp as a guess."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-1-1","title":"2.1.1 — Import and the decoding / .asd habit","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.1 — Import and the decoding / .asd habit","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Live makes an analysis file. Do not panic; do not scatter it.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"When you drag audio in, Live decodes it and can write an <strong><code>.asd</code></strong> analysis file next to the sample (Manual ch. 9 / clip save behavior). That file remembers warp markers and clip settings."},{"type":"prose","html":"Habit: work inside a <strong>Project folder</strong> (0.10). After you finish warping something you care about, Collect All and Save so the audio <em>and</em> its analysis live with the Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> After import, do you know which folder the file came from?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-1-2","title":"2.1.2 — Set tempo vs tap tempo vs clip tempo","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.2 — Set tempo vs tap tempo vs clip tempo","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Three ways to name the pulse. They must agree on purpose.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 9.1:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Set tempo</strong> — Control Bar tempo field. Coarse BPM plus hundredths. Can be automated.  ","<strong>Tap tempo</strong> — click or a mapped key/MIDI once per beat. More taps = more accurate. Optional setting: start playback with tap (four taps in 4/4).  ","<strong>Clip tempo</strong> — a warped clip has its own implied tempo from warp markers. By default warped clips <strong>follow</strong> the Set (9.1.4)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"You can also <strong>nudge</strong> phase (9.1.3) to catch a live musician or a turntable. That is DJ-adjacent; name it, do not live there yet."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Change the Set tempo. Does the warped clip stay on the grid?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-1-3","title":"2.1.3 — Warp markers: first transient is law","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.3 — Warp markers: first transient is law","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Beat 1 sits on 1.1.1 or the whole song is late.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>warp marker</strong> pins a moment in the audio to a moment on Live’s timeline. The first transient you care about — usually the first kick, or the first sung downbeat — belongs on <strong>1.1.1</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"If Auto-Warp guessed the tempo but put the downbeat on 1.1.2, the track is “in time” and still <strong>late</strong>. Fix the first marker before you write a bassline."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Zoom the start. Is the first kick on 1.1.1?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-1-4","title":"2.1.4 — Auto-Warp as a guess you will correct","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.4 — Auto-Warp as a guess you will correct","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Live’s algorithm is a start, not a verdict.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 9.2.5: Auto-Warp usually estimates tempo correctly so the clip syncs. Sometimes it gets tempo right and <strong>downbeat wrong</strong> — drag the first marker. Sometimes you only want Auto-Warp <strong>from here</strong> (from a selected marker to the right). Saved warp markers: Auto-Warp will not overwrite them."},{"type":"prose","html":"Long samples may auto-warp on import (preference: Auto-Warp Long Samples). A factory loop that was already edited to loop cleanly can be told to warp as a loop."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> After Auto-Warp, did you <em>look</em> at bar 1?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-1-5","title":"2.1.5 — Leader and follower — who owns the tempo","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.5 — Leader and follower — who owns the tempo","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Most clips follow the Set. A leader clip can drive the Set.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 9.1.4: by default, warped clips <strong>follow</strong> the Set tempo. Arrangement clips can be set to <strong>Lead</strong>. Then the Set plays at the tempo implied by that clip’s warp markers, as if the clip were unwarped. The Control Bar tempo field deactivates."},{"type":"prose","html":"Any number of clips can be leaders; only one actually drives — the currently playing leader on the <strong>bottom-most</strong> track. Tempo automation is created to track the changes; it is visible on the Main track and not editable there."},{"type":"prose","html":"Producer rule: for original songs in this course, <strong>the Set is the leader</strong>. You tap or type a tempo, then make audio follow. Lead/Follow is for “this recording <em>is</em> the tempo” jobs (a live take, a full mix you are remixing)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Is your Set following a clip, or are clips following the Set? Say which on purpose."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-1-6","title":"2.1.6 — When not to warp","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.6 — When not to warp","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Some audio should stay elastic-off.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Leave Warp <strong>off</strong> when:"},{"type":"list","items":["You want the recording’s original timing as a feature (a loose vocal, a field recording).  ","You will <strong>chop</strong> instead of stretch (1.3).  ","Stretching a full mix in Beats mode is destroying transients you need — turn Warp off until you pick a better mode (1.2)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Unwarped audio ignores the Set tempo. Two unwarped files at different original speeds will drift. That can be music. It is not a mistake until it is accidental."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One clip in a practice Set that is deliberately unwarped."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-1-7","title":"2.1.7 — Two files, one tempo — they must agree","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.7 — Two files, one tempo — they must agree","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> A drum loop and a full mix in the same song share one pulse.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Import a drum loop and a full mix (both legal / owned). They must share a tempo story:"},{"type":"list","items":["Same Set tempo, both warped, first transients on 1.1.1, <strong>or</strong>  ","One is Lead and the other Follows, <strong>or</strong>  ","You have written down why they disagree (a gap, a half-time feel — not an accident)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Practice:</strong> Warp a drum loop and a full mix into the same Set."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Play both. Do the kicks fight?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-1-8","title":"2.1.8 — Metronome as a judge, not a musician","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.1.8 — Metronome as a judge, not a musician","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The click tells the truth. It does not write the song.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Turn the metronome on. If the first kick flams against the click, the marker is wrong — not the click. Then turn the metronome <strong>off</strong> when you write. A click is a judge, not a band member."},{"type":"prose","html":"EDMProd remix guide: after you set Seg. BPM / warp, play with the metronome and confirm the vocal is actually in time."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 2.1 checkpoint:</strong> First kick on 1.1.1."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-2-1","title":"2.2.1 — Beats — drums and sliced rhythm","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.1 — Beats — drums and sliced rhythm","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Preserve transients. Default mode, not a universal mode.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 9.3.1: Beats is best for audio with a <strong>dominant rhythm</strong> — drum loops, most electronic dance loops. Granulation is optimized to preserve transients."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Preserve:</strong> Transients (usually best for percussion) or a grid division. Large grid + transpose can make rhythmic artifacts on purpose."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Transient Loop Mode:</strong> Off (gap/silence between hits), Forward, or Back-and-Forth (often high quality at slower tempos). <strong>Transient Envelope:</strong> 100 = no fade; lower = gating / click control."},{"type":"prose","html":"Live initially defaults to Beats. That does <strong>not</strong> mean every file should stay there."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Would you put a full pop mix here? (No.)"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-2-2","title":"2.2.2 — Tones — monophonic pitched lines","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.2 — Tones — monophonic pitched lines","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> One clear pitch — vocal phrases, bass, a flute.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 9.3.2: distinct pitch — vocals, monophonic instruments, basslines. <strong>Grain Size:</strong> small for clear pitch changes; larger can reduce noise but add artifacts. Grain size follows the audio’s pitch clarity."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> A bass loop: Tones or Beats? If it is a rhythmic 808 pattern, Beats may win. If it is a sung bass, Tones."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-2-3","title":"2.2.3 — Texture — pads and noise beds","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.3 — Texture — pads and noise beds","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> No clear melody — pads, drones, poly beds, noise.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 9.3.3: no clear melody or pitch — polyphonic orchestral, atmospheric pads, noise, drones. Also used for experimental mangling. Grain Size does <strong>not</strong> consider tonal characteristics (unlike Tones). <strong>Fluctuation</strong> adds randomness."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> A pad bed in Beats will chew transients that are not there. Use Texture."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-2-4","title":"2.2.4 — Re-Pitch — speed and pitch locked","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.4 — Re-Pitch — speed and pitch locked","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Old-school / vinyl cousin. Transpose controls turn off.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 9.3.4: changes playback <strong>rate</strong>. Pitch and tempo move together, like a turntable or an old sampler. Double speed → up an octave. Transposition controls are <strong>deactivated</strong> because speed <em>is</em> pitch."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is the cousin of a DJ pitch fader and of hip-hop chipmunk / vinyl-stop colors. It is a <strong>choice</strong>, not the default for a pop vocal you want in key at a new BPM."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> If you need the singer in the same key at a new tempo, Re-Pitch is the wrong mode."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-2-5","title":"2.2.5 — Complex / Complex Pro — mixed audio and songs","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.5 — Complex / Complex Pro — mixed audio and songs","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Standard has these. Use them on full mixes and messy vocals.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 9.3.5: <strong>Complex</strong> is for audio that mixes beats, melodies, and textures — <strong>entire songs</strong>. <strong>Complex Pro</strong> is a higher-quality variation, especially for polyphonic textures or full songs."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Formants</strong> (Pro): at 100%, original formants stay when you transpose — the voice does not become a cartoon as fast. No effect if you do not transpose. <strong>Envelope</strong> (Pro): default 128; lower for high-pitched material, higher for low-pitched."},{"type":"prose","html":"CPU: both can be heavier. Freeze or resample if the Set complains."},{"type":"prose","html":"EDMProd remix #10: most people use Complex or Complex Pro on vocals/stems when remixing. That is this course’s remix default later."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Full mix → Complex or Pro. Drum loop → Beats. Do not reverse those."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-2-6","title":"2.2.6 — A/B the same vocal in two modes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.6 — A/B the same vocal in two modes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Write what you hear. Do not trust the name of the mode.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Same 4 bars of a vocal you own (or a royalty-free phrase). A/B <strong>Tones</strong> vs <strong>Complex Pro</strong>. One sentence each: which smears consonants, which keeps words."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Two sentences in a note."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-2-7","title":"2.2.7 — Off-grid vocals: extra warp markers on transients","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.7 — Off-grid vocals: extra warp markers on transients","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> If the singer is late, mark the words — do not restretch the whole file.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"EDMProd: if the acapella is at a known BPM, enable Warp, set Seg. BPM, use Complex Pro, check the metronome. If it still feels off, <strong>insert warp markers on transients</strong> and move those points. Warning: a marker moves the audio around it. One-off problems may be cleaner as a <strong>chop</strong> (1.3)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One late syllable, one extra marker."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-2-8","title":"2.2.8 — Clip gain, transpose, reverse, fades as arrangement","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.2.8 — Clip gain, transpose, reverse, fades as arrangement","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> These are form tools, not a mix school.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Clip gain, transpose, reverse, and fades change <strong>what the section is</strong>. A reversed cymbal into a downbeat is a transition (Beat Kitchen). A verse down 3 semitones is a form experiment. They are not “mixing.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 2.2 practice:</strong> Same 4 bars, three modes, one sentence each.   <strong>Section 2.2 checkpoint:</strong> Why a full pop mix does not belong in Beats."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-3-1","title":"2.3.1 — Unwarped audio as a musical choice","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.1 — Unwarped audio as a musical choice","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Elastic-off can be the point.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"A loose vocal against a grid can be pocket. A field recording that drifts can be atmosphere. Write the choice. Accidental drift is a bug."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One unwarped clip, one sentence of why."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-3-2","title":"2.3.2 — Tap-to-grid around raw audio","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.2 — Tap-to-grid around raw audio","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Hip-hop flip cousin: metronome vs raw file, you tap the song.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Named secondary: r/makinghiphop unwarped-then-tap. You leave the audio raw and build the <em>grid</em> (or your drum programming) around it. This is a <strong>flip</strong> cousin. You will reuse it in Module 12. Today: know it exists."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not invent a BPM. Tap until the click agrees with the record, or leave the number out."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Could you write drums <em>to</em> the sample instead of stretching the sample <em>to</em> the drums?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-3-3","title":"2.3.3 — Chop instead of stretch when the vocal breaks","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.3 — Chop instead of stretch when the vocal breaks","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> If Complex Pro smears a word, cut the word.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"EDMProd: if a one-off timing problem will not warp cleanly, chop. Section 1.4 (next file) is Slice to Drum Rack. Today: split the clip before the ugly stretch."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One split is better than five desperate markers."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-3-4","title":"2.3.4 — DnB cousin: cut the acapella onto the beat without changing its tempo","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.4 — DnB cousin: cut the acapella onto the beat without changing its tempo","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> EDMProd genre-change note — do not always time-stretch to 174.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"EDMProd remix “change genre”: if an acapella sits at one tempo and you want another, warping can sound strange. A Drum &amp; Bass habit: <strong>cut the acapella onto the beat without changing its tempo</strong>. The words become rhythmic chops at their original speed."},{"type":"prose","html":"Cited DnB band lives on the cheat sheet (170–180 / 174–175). Do not invent a new number. Do not do Amen science (Drums)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Warp vs chop vs leave — write the decision for one phrase."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-3-5","title":"2.3.5 — Two tempos in one Set — only on purpose","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.5 — Two tempos in one Set — only on purpose","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Half-time feel is not two tempos unless you say so.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"A 140 Set with snare on 3 <em>feels</em> like 70. That is still one tempo (local dubstep KB; Beat Kitchen). Two Control Bar tempos, or a leader clip fighting a follower, is a different story. Only on purpose."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Are you in one tempo with a half-time <em>feel</em>, or did you actually change the number?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-3-6","title":"2.3.6 — Write the decision: warp / chop / leave","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.3.6 — Write the decision: warp / chop / leave","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Three legal answers. Accidental is not one of them.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"For every imported file this week, one word in the clip name: <code>WARP</code>, <code>CHOP</code>, or <code>RAW</code>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 2.3 checkpoint:</strong> You have one clip that is deliberately unwarped."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources for Module 1 Part A (author-facing)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Live 12 Standard Manual, ch. 9 (tempo, tap, lead/follow, Auto-Warp, Beats / Tones / Texture / Re-Pitch / Complex / Complex Pro)  ","EDMProd, <em>How to Remix a Song</em> §§ warping stems / acapella, genre-change chop  ","Local KB §2 (Standard includes Complex modes)  ","r/makinghiphop unwarped-then-tap (named secondary)"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Continued in:</strong> <code>Module-01-Chop-MIDI-Law.md</code> (Sections 1.4–1.6 + Gate A)."}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"production-m-2-2","code":"2","title":"Part B: Chop, audio-to-MIDI, law (Gate A)","blurb":"Slicing keeps the original sound and splits time. Convert Melody / Harmony / Drums throws the sound away and keeps guessed notes. Those are different jobs. Mixing them up is how people think Live “didn’t work.” Manual 13.1: slicing “doesn’t","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"production-l-o-5","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"overview","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module 2 — Part B: Chop, audio-to-MIDI, law (Gate A)","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Intent:</strong> Turn audio into <em>playable parts</em> you can re-sequence, then extract <em>notes</em> as a sketch, then refuse illegal sources before you fall in love with a chop.   <strong>Required.</strong> Gate <strong>A</strong> sits at the end of 1.6.   <strong>Do not:</strong> Slice to <strong>Sampler</strong> (Suite). Do not treat Suite <strong>Stem Separation</strong> as a student step. Do not invent BPMs. Do not enable Live’s <strong>MPE</strong> checkbox on the MK4 port.   <strong>Primary sources:</strong> Live 12 Standard Manual ch. 13; ch. 24 (Drum Rack chains); Ableton KB <em>Slicing mode in Drum Racks produce no sound</em>; Ableton <em>Compare editions</em> + Stem Separation FAQ; Launchkey MK4 pad lock (16 pads, notes <strong>36–51</strong>, <strong>channel 10</strong>, C1 = pad 1); KB §9.1 / §9.6; EDMProd <em>How to Remix a Song</em> (2025) tip #12 + remix-vs-bootleg; <em>How to Sample Sounds</em> (Samplesound); <em>Drum Breaks</em> legality chapter; U.S. Copyright Office <em>What Musicians Should Know about Copyright</em>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Slicing <strong>keeps the original sound</strong> and splits time. Convert Melody / Harmony / Drums <strong>throws the sound away</strong> and keeps guessed notes. Those are different jobs. Mixing them up is how people think Live “didn’t work.”"},{"type":"heading","text":"Section 2.4 — Chop, slice, re-sequence","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Slice to <strong>Drum Rack</strong>. Play a new order from the MK4. Treat loop / one-shot / phrase as three different jobs."},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 13.1: slicing “doesn’t analyze the <em>musical</em> context of your original audio. Instead, it simply splits the original audio into portions of time, regardless of the content.” That is the producer’s gift. You are not asking Live what the notes <em>are</em>. You are asking it to give you <strong>handles</strong>."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-4-1","title":"2.4.1 — Loop vs one-shot vs phrase","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.1 — Loop vs one-shot vs phrase","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Three jobs. One file can only be one of them at a time.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Before you slice, name the file’s <strong>job</strong>. <em>How to Sample Sounds</em> (Samplesound, 2025) and the Drum Breaks guide both treat chopping as a decision about <em>purpose</em>, not a button."},{"type":"table","headers":["Job","What it is","Typical Live treatment","Trap"],"rows":[["<strong>Loop</strong>","A repeating cell that already has groove","Warp (1.1–1.2), leave as audio, or slice only if you will <em>re-order</em>","Slicing a loop you meant to leave intact, then fighting the staircase MIDI"],["<strong>One-shot</strong>","A single hit: kick, snare, stab, word","Drop onto a <strong>Drum Rack pad</strong> (Simpler or Drum Sampler in 1-Shot)","Putting a one-shot into Simpler <strong>Slice</strong> mode inside a pad (silence — see 1.4.7)"],["<strong>Phrase</strong>","A 1–4 bar musical sentence (vocal, riff, break)","Warp <em>or</em> <strong>Slice to New MIDI Track</strong> so you can resequence","Stretching a phrase until consonants smear when a chop would have been cleaner (1.3)"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Drum Breaks</em> asks the same question of a break: is this the <strong>foundation</strong> of the track, or <strong>character</strong> on top? Foundation → you will resequence. Character → you may leave it as a loop under or over your own drums."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One owned or royalty-free file. Write <code>LOOP</code>, <code>HIT</code>, or <code>PHRASE</code> in the clip name before you touch Slice."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-4-2","title":"2.4.2 — Slice to New MIDI Track → Drum Rack","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.2 — Slice to New MIDI Track → Drum Rack","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Right-click the clip. Live builds a playable instrument from time.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Select an audio clip. <strong>Create</strong> menu or clip context menu → <strong>Slice to New MIDI Track</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"The slicing dialog (Manual 13.1):"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Division</strong> — beat values, or the clip’s <strong>transients</strong>, or its <strong>Warp Markers</strong>. Transients follow attacks. Warp Markers follow the grid you already argued with in 1.1. Beat divisions ignore content and cut on the ruler.  ","<strong>Slicing Preset</strong> — factory templates, plus any you saved into the User Library default-presets folder. Factory macros typically include envelope, loop, and crossfade for every slice at once.  ","<strong>Preserve warped timing</strong> — <strong>on:</strong> the slices keep the stretch you already applied. <strong>Off:</strong> the sliced version sounds like the <strong>raw</strong> file; warp changes are discarded."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"Hard limit: a Rack can hold <strong>128 chains</strong>. Live will refuse a choice that would make more than 128 slices. Fix: coarser division, or select a <strong>smaller region</strong> of the clip first."},{"type":"prose","html":"After <strong>OK</strong>, Live does three things (this takes a moment — do not mash the button):"},{"type":"list","items":["A <strong>new MIDI track</strong> appears with a MIDI clip. The clip is a chromatic “staircase”: one note per slice, in original order.  ","A <strong>Drum Rack</strong> lands on that track. Each chain is one slice, triggered by one of those notes, loaded into a <strong>Simpler</strong>.  ","The Rack’s <strong>Macro Controls</strong> are pre-mapped from the slicing preset. One Macro moves the same parameter on every Simpler."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"Playing the MIDI clip reconstructs the original order. <strong>Editing the notes</strong> (or swapping pads — 1.4.4) is the new song."},{"type":"prose","html":"Compare-editions: <strong>Audio-slicing</strong> is Standard (and Suite). Intro/Lite do not have it. You have it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> After slice, play the new MIDI clip. Does it replay the phrase in order?"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-4-3","title":"2.4.3 — Why not Sampler (edition lock)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.3 — Why not Sampler (edition lock)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Sampler is Suite. Your chop lives in Drum Rack + Simpler.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton <em>Compare editions</em> (Live 12):"},{"type":"table","headers":["Device / feature","Intro","Standard (this studio)","Suite"],"rows":[["Simpler","yes","<strong>yes</strong>","yes"],["Drum Rack","yes","<strong>yes</strong>","yes"],["Drum Sampler (12.1)","yes","<strong>yes</strong>","yes"],["Audio-slicing","no","<strong>yes</strong>","yes"],["<strong>Sampler</strong>","no","<strong>no</strong>","yes"],["Stem Separation (12.3)","no","<strong>no</strong>","yes"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Some tutorials say “Slice to Sampler.” That path exists only in Suite. This studio’s legal chop is <strong>Slice to New MIDI Track → Drum Rack</strong>, each pad a <strong>Simpler</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Drum Sampler is a fine <em>one-shot</em> player if you drag a single hit onto a pad. The factory Slice command still builds <strong>Simplers</strong>. Do not hunt a Sampler menu. It is not missing. You do not own it."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can say out loud: “I slice to Drum Rack, not Sampler.”"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-4-4","title":"2.4.4 — Resequence: the flip starts here","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.4 — Resequence: the flip starts here","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The staircase is the original. New notes are a new piece.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 13.1.1: the default MIDI is a chromatically-ascending staircase so the chains fire in original order. Two legal ways to destroy that order on purpose:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Edit the MIDI notes.</strong> Delete, move, repeat, leave gaps. A rest is a composition.  ","<strong>Drag Drum Rack pads onto each other</strong> to swap their note mappings. Same notes, different sounds."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"This is where a <strong>flip</strong> begins (Module 13 will finish the definition). A remix keeps a <em>key element</em> identifiable (EDMProd approach #4). A flip may make the source unrecognizable. Resequencing a phrase until the words are in a new order, or the break is a new pattern, is the same family as the jungle/DnB habit in <em>Drum Breaks</em>: chop, rearrange hits, new groove."},{"type":"prose","html":"EDMProd tip #14: “Stems exist to be mangled.” Same verb, legal source only (1.6)."},{"type":"prose","html":"You can also put <strong>MIDI effects</strong> before the Drum Rack (Manual 13.1.2). Arpeggiator and Random on slices are legal experiments. They are not a substitute for deciding the new order with your hands."},{"type":"prose","html":"Per-slice audio effects: each slice is its own chain. Drop a Saturator on <em>one</em> pad. To treat several slices the same, multi-select their chains → <strong>Ctrl+G</strong> to nest a Rack → put the effects after that sub-Rack."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> The MIDI clip no longer looks like a staircase. You can still hear that the <em>sounds</em> came from the phrase."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-4-5","title":"2.4.5 — MK4: 16 slices, notes 36–51, channel 10","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.5 — MK4: 16 slices, notes 36–51, channel 10","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> On the Launchkey MK4 61: 16 slices, notes 36–51, channel 10.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"This studio’s pad lock (Novation MK4 61; same as 0.10):"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>16 pads</strong>, physical <strong>2×8</strong>  ","Drum-mode notes <strong>36–51</strong>  ","MIDI <strong>channel 10</strong>  ","<strong>C1 = pad 1 = note 36</strong>"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Slice more than 16 pieces and the extras live on later notes the 16-pad bank cannot reach without changing octave / bank. For this course: <strong>slice a region that yields 16 or fewer</strong> (8 is the homework in 1.4.10). If you already sliced 32, play the first bank, then decide whether the rest is worth a second Rack."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Drum mode</strong> (KB §5.4): Shift + Pad 10. The pads now speak to the Drum Rack, not Clip launch. Clip mode is still how you launch <em>scenes</em> — do not confuse the two jobs."},{"type":"prose","html":"Confirm in Live: open the sliced Rack, hit pad 1, watch which pad lights. It should be the chain assigned to note 36. If nothing fires, you are in Clip mode, on the wrong track, or MPE is on (1.4.6)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Pad 1 triggers slice 1 (or whichever chain is mapped to 36). You know which."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-4-6","title":"2.4.6 — Pad map + polyphonic aftertouch; MPE off","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.6 — Pad map + polyphonic aftertouch; MPE off","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Draw the 16. Pressure is aftertouch, not MPE.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Chromatic map for this bank (note number = what Live’s MIDI monitor should show on channel 10):"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Physical 2×8 — confirm bottom-left is pad 1 / C1 / 36 by playing it.\n\n  44  45  46  47  48  49  50  51     (often the upper row)\n  36  37  38  39  40  41  42  43     (often the lower row)\n\n  C1=36  C#1  D1  D#1  E1  F1  F#1  G1\n  G#1=44 A1   A#1 B1   C2  C#2 D2   D#2=51</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"If your hardware row is flipped, <strong>the numbers win</strong>. Write the layout you actually see on a sticky note in the Project folder. Do not memorize a YouTuber’s Push map."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Aftertouch, not MPE.</strong> Novation: MK4 pads are velocity-sensitive with <strong>polyphonic aftertouch</strong> (FSR pads). The keys do <strong>not</strong> send aftertouch. CDM’s MK4 hands-on states the same: poly AT on pads, not on keys, and not MPE."},{"type":"prose","html":"Live’s <strong>MPE</strong> checkbox on the MK4 MIDI port stays <strong>off</strong>. MPE expects a channel-per-note world. Turning it on can monopolize channels and <strong>break channel-10 Drum Racks</strong> (course lock; beginner-mistake canon #9)."},{"type":"prose","html":"MK4 Settings (hardware): Pad Aftertouch <strong>Off / Channel / Poly</strong>. For this chop week:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Off</strong> if pressure is accidentally opening filters you did not map.  ","<strong>Poly</strong> if you have mapped pad pressure to something musical <em>and</em> Live MPE is still off.  ","<strong>Channel</strong> (channel aftertouch) is one pressure value for the whole pad bank — useful, still not MPE."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Live MPE on the MK4 port is unchecked. You have a 16-cell drawing with 36 in the pad-1 cell."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-4-7","title":"2.4.7 — Simpler vs Rack pad — which job","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.7 — Simpler vs Rack pad — which job","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> One sample across keys, or one sample per pad. Do not nest Slice mode in a pad.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Three legal instruments, three jobs:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Tool","Job","When"],"rows":[["<strong>Simpler Classic</strong> on its <strong>own MIDI track</strong>","One file, playable across the keyboard (pitch follows notes)","You turned a vocal <em>note</em> or a piano hit into an instrument (<em>How to Sample Sounds</em>: “turning samples into new instruments”)"],["<strong>Simpler 1-Shot</strong> or <strong>Drum Sampler</strong> on a <strong>Drum Rack pad</strong>","One hit, ignore note length","Kicks, snares, stabs, single words"],["<strong>Slice to New MIDI Track</strong> (many Simplers inside a Drum Rack)","Many time-slices, one pad each","Resequencing a phrase or break"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton KB <em>Slicing mode in Drum Racks produce no sound</em>: a Drum Rack pad sends <strong>one MIDI note</strong> into whatever sits on that pad. Simpler in <strong>Classic</strong> or <strong>1-Shot</strong> is happy. Simpler in <strong>Slice</strong> mode expects a <em>range</em> of notes. One note arrives → often <strong>silence</strong> or a random slice. Fix: put Slice-mode Simpler on its <strong>own track</strong>, or right-click Simpler → <strong>Convert / Slice to Drum Rack</strong> so each slice gets its own pad."},{"type":"prose","html":"Do not drop a Slice-mode Simpler onto pad 36 and wonder why the MK4 is dead."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can point at your device chain and say which of the three jobs it is."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-4-8","title":"2.4.8 — Reverse, re-pitch, reorder as arrangement","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.8 — Reverse, re-pitch, reorder as arrangement","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Clip and pad edits are form tools, not a mix school.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Same family as 1.2.8. Now they live on <em>pads</em>."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Reverse</strong> a slice (Simpler / clip) to make a transition into a downbeat (Beat Kitchen cymbal-reverse cousin).  ","<strong>Transpose / re-pitch</strong> a slice to answer a chorus or to fake a new word.  ","<strong>Reorder</strong> (1.4.4) to write a new rhythm.  ","<strong>Per-pad volume / pan</strong> (Drum Rack chain controls, Manual 24.4) to make verse pads quieter than chorus pads — KB §9.4 density lever, not “mixing class.”"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>How to Sample Sounds</em>: pitch shift, time stretch, EQ, then effects. Stretch belongs in 1.1–1.2. Chop week prefers <strong>cut, reverse, re-pitch, reorder</strong>. If you need the whole file slower, you probably wanted Warp, not 32 tiny pads."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One reversed slice used as a pickup into bar 1 of a new pattern."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-4-9","title":"2.4.9 — Name the clips so future-you can arrange","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.9 — Name the clips so future-you can arrange","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> <code>CHOP_vox_hook_legal</code> survives a month. <code>MIDI 3</code> does not.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Rename (Manual 7.1):"},{"type":"list","items":["The <strong>audio</strong> source clip: <code>PHRASE_hook_rf</code> or <code>BREAK_think_owned</code> (job + what + legal status).  ","The <strong>MIDI</strong> staircase clip, after you edit it: <code>FLIP_hook_v1</code>.  ","The <strong>Drum Rack</strong> itself: <code>DR_hook_8</code>.  ","Chains / pads you will solo later: <code>kickish</code>, <code>word_you</code>, <code>breath</code>."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"1.6 will add a written source log. Naming is the log’s twin. If you cannot tell a legal chop from a scratch import by reading the Session grid, you will grab the wrong file in Module 12."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> No clip created this week is still called <code>MIDI</code> or <code>1-Audio</code>."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-4-10","title":"2.4.10 — Royalty-free 2-bar phrase → 8 pads → new pattern","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.4.10 — Royalty-free 2-bar phrase → 8 pads → new pattern","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Hardware re-sequence of a legal phrase.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Practice (section):</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Import a <strong>royalty-free</strong> (or self-recorded) ~2-bar phrase. Not a ripped radio vocal.  ","First transient on <strong>1.1.1</strong>. Warp only if you need it; chop can work raw.  ","Slice to New MIDI Track. Division: transients, or 8th notes if transients explode past 16. Aim for <strong>8 pads</strong> you will actually play.  ","MK4 Drum mode. Play a <strong>new</strong> 1- or 2-bar pattern. It must not be the original order.  ","Name everything (1.4.9)."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 2.4 checkpoint:</strong> You can play the new pattern from the 16 pads without looking at the mouse."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-5-1","title":"2.5.1 — Three starting doors: groove / harmony / topline","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.1 — Three starting doors: groove / harmony / topline","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Pick one door per song. Convert is the topline door’s hinge.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §9.1 — pick <strong>one</strong> start per song:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Door","How in Live","Good when"],"rows":[["<strong>Groove</strong>","Drum Rack + bass","Dance, hip-hop, band feel"],["<strong>Harmony</strong>","Chord clip + Scale/Chord MIDI FX","Ballads, pop, singer-songwriter"],["<strong>Melody / topline</strong>","Hum into audio → Audio to MIDI, or play lead","Hook-first writers"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Convert Melody serves the <strong>topline</strong> door. Convert Harmony serves the <strong>harmony</strong> door when you recorded (or own) a guitar/piano take. Convert Drums is a <em>sketch</em> of a groove — the Drums course owns the real grid."},{"type":"prose","html":"You do not run all three on the same file “to be thorough.” Isolated material, one command (13.5)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> This week’s sketch has one named door on a sticky note."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-5-2","title":"2.5.2 — Convert Melody","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.2 — Convert Melody","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Monophonic audio → notes + a preview synth.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 13.3: identifies pitches in <strong>monophonic</strong> audio. New MIDI track. Preloaded Instrument Rack: a synth whose <strong>Synth to Piano</strong> Macro morphs analog-style synth ↔ electric piano. Replace it."},{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton’s intended sources: a song in your collection, <strong>or you singing, whistling, or playing a solo instrument</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Right-click the audio clip → <strong>Convert Melody to New MIDI Track</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"If the line is a chordal guitar, you asked the wrong command (use Harmony). If it is a full mix, you asked a drunk intern to transcribe a band."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> One hummed or whistled phrase becomes a MIDI clip you can retune."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-5-3","title":"2.5.3 — Convert Harmony","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.3 — Convert Harmony","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Polyphonic audio → a chord sketch + a piano Rack.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 13.2: identifies pitches in a <strong>polyphonic</strong> recording. New MIDI track. Preloaded Instrument Rack plays <strong>piano</strong>. Replace it."},{"type":"prose","html":"Intended sources: music from your collection, <strong>or you playing guitar or piano</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 13.5 “wrong command” trick: Convert Harmony on a <strong>monophonic</strong> vocal often invents notes that were never there, because the algorithm wants chords. That can be a writing spark. It is not a transcription. Label the clip <code>HARM_wrongcmd_spark</code> if you do this on purpose."},{"type":"prose","html":"EDMProd #12 screenshot path matches: right-click → Convert Harmony for polyphonic, Convert Melody for monophonic."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> A guitar or piano take you own (or a royalty-free loop) becomes a chord clip you then correct by ear."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-5-4","title":"2.5.4 — Convert Drums — starting point only","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.4 — Convert Drums — starting point only","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Starting point only. The Drums course owns the grid.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 13.4: extracts rhythms from <strong>unpitched, percussive</strong> audio. Tries to sort <strong>kick / snare / hi-hat</strong> onto a preloaded Drum Rack."},{"type":"prose","html":"Works well on recorded <strong>breakbeats</strong>, beatboxing, tapping a table. Works badly on a full pop mix (13.5: other instruments get detected as notes)."},{"type":"prose","html":"You may <strong>tune transients</strong> on the audio clip <em>before</em> converting. Live uses those markers as note divisions (13.5). Adding/moving/deleting transients is how you “tune” the guess."},{"type":"prose","html":"This is <strong>not</strong> a drum-programming degree. Do not spend an evening velocity-humanizing a Convert Drums roll and call it Level 4 of the Drums course. Clean obvious doubles, keep the feel if it is good, move on. Groove-door writing still means <em>you</em> place kicks on purpose later."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You used Convert Drums on an isolated break or a table-tap — not on a mastered song."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-5-5","title":"2.5.5 — Clean the roll; assign a stock instrument","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.5 — Clean the roll; assign a stock instrument","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The guess is a draft. You are the copy-editor.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual 13.5 — better conversions:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Clear attacks.</strong> Swells and pads confuse the detector.  ","<strong>Isolated instruments.</strong> Convert Drums wants an unaccompanied break.  ","<strong>Uncompressed .wav / .aiff.</strong> Low-bitrate MP3s are “unpredictable unless high bitrate.”  ","<strong>Tune transients first.</strong>"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Then do the human work EDMProd and KB §9.6 both require:"},{"type":"list","items":["Delete octave errors and ghost notes that are not in the performance.  ","Fix obviously wrong chord tones against a tuner or your ear.  ","<strong>Replace</strong> the preview Rack with a Core Library instrument you would actually ship (0.10.10: default sounds are legal finishers).  ","Quantize <strong>after</strong>, and not always 100% (KB §9.5) — strength is a feel choice, reused in 2.2."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"Tabs / published chords (EDMProd #12 fallback) are a <em>check</em>, not a license to paste a whole song you do not own."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> The MIDI clip has fewer notes than Live dumped, and a named stock instrument."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-5-6","title":"2.5.6 — Hum → warp → MIDI → clip in a named scene","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.5.6 — Hum → warp → MIDI → clip in a named scene","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The non-pianist recipe, end to end.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"KB §9.6 as a procedure:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Hum</strong> (or whistle, or guitar) onto an audio track.  ","<strong>Warp roughly</strong> to the Set tempo. First sung downbeat on 1.1.1 if there is one.  ","<strong>Convert</strong> Melody (or Harmony if you played chords).  ","<strong>Clean</strong> the roll (1.5.5).  ","Drop the MIDI clip into a Session slot. <strong>Name the scene</strong> (<code>Hook</code> / <code>Topline</code>) — not <code>scene 3</code>."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"You now have a clip that can sit in a song later. You do not have a song. Loop-trap still applies (0.3)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 2.5 checkpoint:</strong> One hummed hook exists as cleaned MIDI in a named scene."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-6-1","title":"2.6.1 — Consent vs “everyone samples”","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.6.1 — Consent vs “everyone samples”","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Culture is not a license. Two copyrights sit on most records.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"U.S. Copyright Office, <em>What Musicians Should Know about Copyright</em>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"When you record a song you may create <strong>two</strong> protected works, commonly owned and licensed <strong>separately</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Musical work</strong> — composition + lyrics (usually the songwriter / publisher).  ","<strong>Sound recording</strong> — the fixed performance (usually the label / performer / producer)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"To use someone else’s recording <strong>or</strong> composition you generally must: use a <strong>public-domain</strong> work, <strong>get permission / a license</strong>, or fit a <strong>statutory exception</strong> (the Office names fair use and the §115 musical-work license — §115 is a <em>composition</em> mechanical path, <strong>not</strong> a free pass to lift a master)."},{"type":"prose","html":"The Office is blunt: <strong>there is no hard minimum amount</strong> of music you can take without permission when you need it. Trying and failing to reach the owner is <strong>not</strong> permission."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Drum Breaks</em> opens the same door correctly: “you are never allowed to use other people’s music without their given consent.” Then it adds that classic-break culture is so common you are “unlikely to run into legal trouble.” That second sentence is <strong>history, not a classroom license</strong>. Amen, Think, Apache, Funky Drummer are famous <strong>because</strong> they were sampled — not because they are free."},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>How to Sample Sounds</em>: a recognizable portion may need <strong>clearance</strong>; otherwise takedowns or legal action."},{"type":"prose","html":"“Everyone samples” describes a scene. It does not sign the two copyrights over to you."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can name the two copyrights on a typical commercial record."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-6-2","title":"2.6.2 — Royalty-free as the safe path for this course","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.6.2 — Royalty-free as the safe path for this course","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Packs with a license you can read. Or you recorded it.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>How to Sample Sounds</em> and <em>Drum Breaks</em> agree on the beginner path: <strong>royalty-free</strong> (or public-domain) libraries."},{"type":"prose","html":"Safe for every deliverable in this school:"},{"type":"list","items":["You <strong>recorded</strong> it (hum, guitar, table, field).  ","A pack whose <strong>license you opened</strong> (Core Library, a store pack, a label remix pack).  ","<strong>Public domain</strong> where you have actually checked <em>both</em> composition and recording (a 1920 composition is not the same as a 2020 performance of it)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Not safe as a <em>course</em> source: YouTube rips, Spotify stems you did not buy, Discord “acapella packs,” vinyl you digitized from a store-bought LP <strong>if you plan to distribute</strong> the result as yours. Private study of a record you own is a different question than handing this school a listen file built from a ripped master."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> This week’s chop and Convert files are from a named legal bucket."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-6-3","title":"2.6.3 — Official remix packs vs bootleg stereo rips","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.6.3 — Official remix packs vs bootleg stereo rips","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> A pack is an invitation. A rip is a problem.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Official remix pack / stems / acapella</strong> — the rightsholder (or a contest they authorized) handed you files. Read the <strong>term</strong>: many contests expire; EDMProd says using leftover contest stems after the window is still a <strong>bootleg</strong> if you distribute. The pack is not “yours forever” unless the license says so."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Bootleg stereo rip</strong> — a full mix (or a rip someone labeled “acapella”) with no grant. EDMProd: if you edit a song and <strong>distribute it anywhere</strong> without permission, it is a bootleg."},{"type":"prose","html":"You may <strong>study</strong> a mix you own (warp practice in 1.1 used owned files). You may <strong>not</strong> treat a rip as the source for Deliverable 3 or 4."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can point at a file and say “pack + license” or “rip.” No third category called “I found it.”"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-6-4","title":"2.6.4 — You may not have stems — plan B is a clean breakdown or acapella you own","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.6.4 — You may not have stems — plan B is a clean breakdown or acapella you own","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Plan B is a clean breakdown or acapella you own.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"EDMProd tip #4 (bootlegs, as <em>craft</em> not as permission): if you lack stems, you pick sections of the original that are <strong>sparse enough</strong> to use — often a breakdown, rarely a dense chorus."},{"type":"prose","html":"This course’s plan B is stricter than EDMProd’s SoundCloud shrug:"},{"type":"list","items":["An <strong>official</strong> acapella or breakdown stem, or  ","A <strong>clean breakdown / acapella you recorded or licensed</strong>, or  ","You <strong>do not</strong> make that remix and you pick another legal source."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"You do not “make stems” with Suite Stem Separation (1.6.5). You do not download a random “acapella” whose waveform is a full mix with the vocal loud."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Written plan B for one song you like: official pack / own recording / skip that song."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-6-5","title":"2.6.5 — Suite Stem Separation is not a lesson step","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.6.5 — Suite Stem Separation is not a lesson step","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Standard does not have it. We will not pretend.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Ableton official:"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Compare editions</em>: <strong>Stem Separation</strong> = Suite only (new in Live 12.3). Standard = <strong>×</strong>.  ","Stem Separation FAQ: “It is not available in Live Standard, Intro, or Lite.”  ","Live 12.3 blog: Suite and Push 3 Standalone split vocals / bass / drums / other from any clip."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"Even if you later buy Suite: this course <strong>will not</strong> list Stem Separation as a required or optional student step. Two reasons:"},{"type":"list","items":["Edition lock — we teach what this studio owns.  ","Ethics — a magic “split this Billboard record” button is not consent (1.6.1). A separated vocal is still <strong>their recording</strong>."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"Plan B (1.6.4) is the craft. Third-party splitter sites are the same legal object as a rip plus a worse file."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can state, in one sentence, why Stem Separation is not a step <strong>here</strong>."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-6-6","title":"2.6.6 — Browser / Places / Collections for your legal library","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.6.6 — Browser / Places / Collections for your legal library","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> The library is a place, not a pile on the Desktop.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Manual / course hygiene (0.10): Project folder + Collect All and Save."},{"type":"prose","html":"Add a <strong>Place</strong> in Live’s Browser that points at <strong>your</strong> royalty-free / own-recordings folder — not at a downloads dump that also holds rips. <strong>Collections</strong> (colors) can mark <code>legal-hooks</code>, <code>legal-breaks</code>, <code>own-vox</code>."},{"type":"prose","html":"If a file is not in that Place, it is not in this course’s palette."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Browser shows one Place you could explain to a lawyer as “this is what I am allowed to use.”"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-6-7","title":"2.6.7 — Name clips so future-you can arrange","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.6.7 — Name clips so future-you can arrange","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Legal status in the name, not in your memory.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Same discipline as 1.4.9, now with a <strong>rights tag</strong>:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Tag","Meaning"],"rows":[["<code>OWN</code>","You recorded it"],["<code>RF</code>","Royalty-free pack, license on file"],["<code>PACK</code>","Official remix / stem pack, terms read"],["<code>PD</code>","Public domain — you checked both copyrights"],["<code>NO</code>","Study-only owned mix — <strong>do not</strong> chop into a deliverable"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Example: <code>PHRASE_hook_RF_packname</code>. The source log (1.6.10) stores the URL / pack / date. The clip name stores the verdict."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Every imported clip this week has a rights tag."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-6-8","title":"2.6.8 — Grey-area honesty — what this school will not instruct","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.6.8 — Grey-area honesty — what this school will not instruct","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> We will name the grey. We will not give you a map through it.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Honest grey (teach as <em>literacy</em>, not as how-to):"},{"type":"list","items":["Classic break culture (<em>Drum Breaks</em>: “unlikely to run into legal trouble”).  ","<em>Drum Breaks</em> also suggests processing/layering “to avoid copyright issues.” Processing is <strong>not</strong> a legal eraser. The Copyright Office: no minimum amount.  ","EDMProd: most bootleggers are not sued; SoundCloud “99% of the time nothing will happen”; Spotify/YouTube/Apple may strike you. That is <strong>risk gossip</strong>, not a policy.  ","WIPO / national laws differ (Canada has a non-commercial user-generated-content exception with tests; U.S. fair use is a four-factor court question). You do not want your first court case to be the lesson."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>This school will not instruct:</strong> ripping, cracking, using unofficial stem sites, uploading bootlegs “because SoundCloud,” or “just mash it until it is fair use.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"Private listening and private practice on music you <strong>bought</strong> is your home. Distribution and course deliverables use the safe path (1.6.2)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> You can explain the grey without asking for a workaround."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-6-9","title":"2.6.9 — EDMProd remix vs bootleg (preview of Module 12)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.6.9 — EDMProd remix vs bootleg (preview of Module 12)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> Remix is a method. Bootleg is a permission problem.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"EDMProd (Sam Matla, 2025), with their lawyer disclaimer attached:"},{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Note","html":"A bootleg is any form of <em>illegal</em> or unauthorized remix or edit. If you edit a song and distribute it online or anywhere else without permission, it’s a bootleg."},{"type":"prose","html":"Their examples of bootlegs: a studio acapella you were handed + a SoundCloud upload with no clearance; contest stems used <strong>after</strong> the contest."},{"type":"prose","html":"Their fair-use note: unofficial remixes, mashups, edits, and anything using the <strong>existing recording</strong> generally need permission to sell <strong>or even freely distribute</strong>. Fair use examples they list (criticism, commentary, reporting, teaching, research) are not “I made a club edit.”"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>This course’s later split (locked):</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Remix (Deliverable 3, Gate F):</strong> identity remains — EDMProd “key element” still identifiable. Legal source in writing.  ","<strong>Flip (Deliverable 4, Gate G):</strong> identity may disappear. Still needs a legal source. Not songs 1–3."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"A bootleg is not a third artistic method. It is an unauthorized <strong>release</strong>. You will practice both <em>methods</em> on files you are allowed to touch."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Check:</strong> Remix / flip / bootleg — three sentences, no synonyms."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"production-l1-6-10","title":"2.6.10 — Written source log habit","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"2.6.10 — Written source log habit","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Short description:</strong> One line per imported file, forever.  "},{"type":"prose","html":"Keep a text file in the Project folder (<code>SOURCE-LOG.txt</code> or a note clip). One line per import:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">date | clip name | origin (pack / own / PD) | license or “self” | URL or disk path | used in (sketch / D3 / D4 / study-only)</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"If you cannot fill the origin column, the file does not enter a deliverable."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 2.6 practice:</strong> Source log has at least the 1.4 phrase and the 1.5 hum."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Section 2.6 checkpoint — Gate A</strong> (self-assessment):"},{"type":"table","headers":["#","Pass means"],"rows":[["1","A loop (or phrase) is warped with beat 1 on <strong>1.1.1</strong>"],["2","That material (or another legal phrase) is <strong>sliced to a Drum Rack</strong> and playable"],["3","You can name <strong>Beats</strong> vs <strong>Complex / Complex Pro</strong> and say why a full pop mix does not belong in Beats (1.2)"],["4","You can state why <strong>Stem Separation is not a step</strong> (Suite-only + not consent)"],["5","MK4 <strong>MPE off</strong>; pads <strong>36–51 / ch. 10</strong>"],["6","Source log exists; no deliverable file is a rip"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"No app can watch Ableton. You tick the boxes. Then Section A (Session) starts."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Sources for Module 1 Part B (author-facing)","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Ableton Live 12 Standard Manual, ch. 13 (Slice to New MIDI Track; Convert Harmony / Melody / Drums; optimization)  ","Ableton Live 12 Standard Manual, ch. 24.1–24.4 (Drum Rack: one note per chain; pad view; chain volume/pan)  ","Ableton Live 12 Standard Manual, ch. 7.1–7.5 (rename; scenes — reused in Module 2)  ","Ableton KB, <em>Slicing mode in Drum Racks produce no sound</em>  ","Ableton, <em>Compare Live editions</em> (Sampler Suite-only; Stem Separation Suite 12.3; Audio-slicing + Audio to MIDI on Standard; Simpler / Drum Rack / Drum Sampler owned)  ","Ableton Help, <em>Stem Separation in Ableton Live FAQ</em>  ","Novation Launchkey MK4 product specs + CDM MK4 hands-on (pads: polyphonic aftertouch, not MPE; not on keys)  ","Songwriting KB §9.1 (three doors), §9.5 (velocity / quantize), §9.6 (hum → warp → Convert)  ","EDMProd, <em>How to Remix a Song</em> (2025), tips #12, #14 + remix-vs-bootleg / fair-use  ","Samplesound, <em>How to Sample Sounds: A Beginner’s Guide for Music Producers</em> (28 Apr 2025)  ","<em>Drum Breaks: A Guide To Injecting Character into Your Music</em> (definition + legality + chop-to-new-pattern)  ","U.S. Copyright Office, <em>What Musicians Should Know about Copyright</em> (two works; no minimum use; permission ≠ failed contact)"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Next file:</strong> Section A begins — <code>Module-02-Session.md</code> (clips, scenes, Capture, perform-as-document)."}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
